# Viberr environment configuration.
# Copy to .env for local development, or for a Docker host that holds real data
# (.env is gitignored — never commit it). A bare `docker compose up` needs no
# .env at all (ruling 504). All variables are validated at startup by
# app/server/config/env.server.ts; the server refuses to boot with a clear
# message if anything is invalid.

# ============ Secrets (generated when unset) ============
#
# Leave both unset and the first process that reads the env generates them,
# once, into <VIBERR_DATA_ROOT>/state/instance-secrets.json (0600, the server's
# own; ruling 504), and every process reads them back. A backup carries that
# file. A value set here wins, key by key. Once anything has been sealed, take
# the generated values over unchanged (copy them out of that file); a new key
# needs the rotation below, or every sealed secret becomes unreadable.

# Signs the session cookie. Random string, at least 32 characters.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 48
#VIBERR_SESSION_SECRET=

# AES-256-GCM key for every secret stored in the database: GitHub PATs, MCP
# credentials, OAuth client secrets, the S3 audit-export key and each person's
# pasted agent-backend key. Losing it makes all of them unreadable.
# Must be base64 that decodes to EXACTLY 32 bytes.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
#VIBERR_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ============ Docker Compose (read by compose.yml, not the app) ============

# A CPU ceiling for the container, roughly (cores - 3) on a machine someone also
# works on: one agent run can fork a test worker per CPU it sees. Unset, there is
# none. Docker refuses to start the container with a value above the host's CPUs.
#VIBERR_CPUS=7

# ============ Optional ============

# Key-rotation window: when you rotate VIBERR_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY, set this to
# the OLD key (or a comma-separated list of retired keys, newest first) so
# already-encrypted secrets still DECRYPT during the changeover. Reads only —
# new writes use the current key. Read straight from the environment (not the
# validated schema) so the rotation is operational, not a boot-time config, and
# a malformed entry is skipped in silence (a key list must never surface in an
# error message). `npm run keys -- status` counts the secrets still sealed
# under a retired key; `npm run keys -- reseal` (app stopped) moves them. Remove
# this once status reports none.
# VIBERR_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Runtime data root: canonical project/task files, sqlite projections, run
# logs. Relative paths resolve against the working directory. Default: ./data
#
# SET IT, and set it to ./docker-data. That is the root this project develops
# against with `npm run dev`, and the one .claude/launch.json exports. It is NOT
# the container's store any more (ruling 460): compose.yml mounts the named
# volume `viberr-data` at /data, because a macOS bind mount enforces no file
# permissions between users and every agent now runs as its person's own user.
# `npm run store:to-volume` moves a store from ./docker-data into that volume
# once (docs/operations/deployment.md). Leaving this unset (or on the ./data
# default) does NOT fail — the app cheerfully boots a SECOND, empty-or-stale
# store, and the symptom is a workspace with the wrong projects and missing PATs
# and run logs, never an error message.
#
# It must not disagree with .claude/launch.json. A `npm run dev` that reads a
# different root than the launcher does is how you end up running two app
# processes against one store without noticing when they later converge — the
# dual-writer hazard the writer lock below exists to stop, which over VirtioFS
# has already clobbered a WAL.
VIBERR_DATA_ROOT=./docker-data

# ONE app process per data root, ever: boot takes an exclusive lock at
# <VIBERR_DATA_ROOT>/state/writer.lock and a second process refuses to start,
# naming the holder; the writing CLIs (seed, rescan, restore, keys reseal) take
# the same lock. A lock left by a process on ANOTHER host (a container that no
# longer exists) cannot be probed from here — set this to 1 (or true/yes) for
# one boot to take it over. Default: off.
#VIBERR_FORCE_DATA_ROOT_LOCK=1

# Ruling 636: where each agent run's own temporary directory (its TMPDIR) is
# made, removed when the run settles. Default: viberr-runs under the server's
# temp directory.
#VIBERR_RUN_TMP_ROOT=/tmp/viberr-runs

# HTTP port for the dev server and react-router-serve. Default: 5173 for
# `npm run dev`. The image sets 3000, and compose publishes ${PORT:-3000} on both
# sides, so setting it here moves both. `npm run deploy` verifies the new
# container on whichever port that gives.
#PORT=5173

# development | production | test. Default: development
#NODE_ENV=development

# Absolute public origin of the app, e.g. https://viberr.example.com. better-auth
# uses it to build OAuth callback + cookie URLs (and PR back-links). Optional in
# dev (the origin is inferred from the request); REQUIRED behind a reverse proxy
# so redirects and cookies resolve to the public host. Use the https:// origin:
# an http:// one in production issues session cookies without Secure (boot warns).
#BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# better-auth cookie-signing secret. Optional — defaults to VIBERR_SESSION_SECRET.
# Set it only to rotate the auth secret independently of the legacy session
# secret. Random string, at least 32 characters. Generate: openssl rand -base64 48
#BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Log verbosity: debug | info | warn | error. Read directly off the environment
# by the (dependency-free) logger — NOT validated by env.server.ts.
# Default: info in production, debug otherwise.
#LOG_LEVEL=debug

# GitHub OAuth login — optional; the login button stays disabled when unset.
#GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Google OAuth login — optional; the login button stays disabled when unset.
#GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Seed admin: created automatically (at boot, or by `npm run seed`) when the
# users table is EMPTY. Without these, the default admin is admin@viberr.dev —
# at boot with a random one-time password printed ONCE to stdout (marked
# "VIBERR BOOTSTRAP ADMIN"; must set a new password at first sign-in), or via
# `npm run seed` with the known dev default password it prints.
# Password must be at least 8 characters.
#VIBERR_SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
#VIBERR_SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-please

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent backends (Claude, Codex) are connected per person on Profile → Agent
# accounts; nothing to set here (ruling 127).
#
# Each person signs in through the bundled vendor binary, or pastes their own
# API key or ChatGPT workspace access token. A pasted key is sealed with
# VIBERR_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY above; a sign-in stays in that person's own
# runtime home under <data root>/runtimes/users/. Every run bills the ONE
# person it is for: the task owner on a task, you on your own controller
# conversation.
#
# --- Runtime tuning (all optional) ---
# Parsed at boot (ruling 458(j)): unset takes the default, and a value that does
# not parse stops the app with "Invalid environment configuration" instead of
# running the default.
#
#   Idle (inactivity) timeout for a Codex run, in ms — the window a single
#   turn/tool may produce no event before the run is treated as hung.
#   Default: 900000 (15 minutes).
#VIBERR_CODEX_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
#
#   The same hang guard for a CLAUDE run (P13-RT-11 — Claude runs had none, so a
#   stalled run held the delivering single-flight until the next restart).
#   Default: 900000 (15 minutes).
#VIBERR_CLAUDE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
#
#   Turn cap for a Claude run — a runaway guard, not a work budget. A run
#   that hits it is CUT OFF and surfaces as run·error·max_turns. 1 or more;
#   a fraction rounds down.
#   Default: 2000.
#VIBERR_CLAUDE_MAX_TURNS=2000
#
#   Ceiling on a single `git clone` / mirror fetch, in ms. It exists to stop a
#   HUNG clone, not to rule on how big a repository may be — a first clone of a
#   large repo legitimately takes many minutes, and the scheduler's claim lease
#   is sized against this value.
#   Default: 900000 (15 minutes).
#VIBERR_GIT_CLONE_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
#
#   Age (in days) at which a raw run transcript under runtimes/<backend>/ is
#   pruned. Aligned with the run-log projection window so the file and its
#   projection disappear together. Set 0 to keep transcripts forever.
#   Default: 30.
#VIBERR_TRANSCRIPT_RETENTION_DAYS=30
#
#   The same window for the provider SESSION homes the app owns, now one set
#   per person (runtimes/users/<userId>/claude-home/projects and
#   .../codex-home/sessions, *.jsonl only). Deleting one ends resume/export for
#   that conversation; the providers keep roughly 30 days themselves. Sign-in
#   files are never touched. Set 0 to keep them forever.
#   Default: 30.
#VIBERR_SESSION_HOME_RETENTION_DAYS=30
#
#   Number of trusted reverse proxies in front of the app. The login throttle
#   reads X-Forwarded-For ONLY when this is set, and then as the Nth hop from
#   the right (the ip the outermost trusted proxy saw). Leave unset for the
#   shipped proxy-less deployment, where the header is pure client input and
#   trusting it would let a brute-forcer bypass the per-email throttle.
#   Default: 0 (trust none).
#VIBERR_TRUST_PROXY=1

# --- Controller configuration locks (ruling 108) ---------------------------
# The controller's grant sections and instructions are LOCKED by default, org
# admins included: the Controller settings tab shows them read-only and a save
# that changes them is refused. Set a variable below to "enabled" to unlock ONE
# section for in-app editing (here or in the deployment environment), then
# restart; "disabled" (or any other value, or unset) keeps it locked. Model
# and effort are always editable, and the built-in viberr_ops diagnostics
# mount is never removable regardless of these variables.
#VIBERR_UNLOCK_CONTROLLER_SKILLS=enabled
#VIBERR_UNLOCK_CONTROLLER_KB=enabled
#VIBERR_UNLOCK_CONTROLLER_MCPS=enabled
#VIBERR_UNLOCK_CONTROLLER_INSTRUCTIONS=enabled

# --- Ops knobs (all optional) ---
# The maintenance, disk-space and write-probe knobs are parsed at boot (ruling
# 458(c)): unset takes the default, and a value that does not parse stops the
# app with "Invalid environment configuration" instead of running the default.
# Period of the maintenance pass (audit retention + export, transcript
# pruning), in SECONDS, at most 86400 (24 hours). Default 21600 (6 hours).
# Ruling 458(i): the ms-valued VIBERR_MAINTENANCE_INTERVAL_MS it replaces is
# refused at boot.
#VIBERR_MAINTENANCE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=21600
# Period of the free-space check on the data root, in SECONDS, at most 86400.
# Default 300 (5 minutes). Replaces VIBERR_DISK_CHECK_INTERVAL_MS, likewise
# refused at boot.
#VIBERR_DISK_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300
# Free-space thresholds behind health's disk.status, in MB: below LOW the
# status is `low`, below CRITICAL `critical`; either marks the instance
# degraded and triggers an extra maintenance pass. Defaults 2048 / 512. The
# reading itself comes from `df -kP` (fragment size aware) with statfs as the
# fallback.
#VIBERR_DISK_LOW_FREE_MB=2048
#VIBERR_DISK_CRITICAL_FREE_MB=512
# A directory on the host disk under the data root, measured beside it; the
# tighter of the two is the reading (ruling 603). compose.yml sets /host-disk,
# its read-only mount of ./.host-disk, because Docker Desktop's volume reports
# the VM disk image's virtual size, not the host's free space. On a Linux host
# whose Docker storage is not on the checkout's disk, set it empty to measure
# the data root alone.
#VIBERR_HOST_DISK_PATH=/host-disk
# Opt PAT validation into the empty-payload write dry-run (1/true/yes). Unset
# (or 0/false/no), write access is proved read-only from the repository's
# permissions block.
#VIBERR_GITHUB_WRITE_PROBE=1
# Path of the browser executable the use-browser capability launches; the
# image bakes /usr/bin/chromium (and the browser MCP then also gets
# --no-sandbox). Unset on a dev host, Playwright's own browser resolution
# applies. Set only to point at another build.
#VIBERR_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/chromium
# Port of the loopback MCP gateway (bound to 127.0.0.1 only) through which a
# run reaches an org MCP server that has a stored credential; the credential
# stays in the server (ruling 461). 0, the default, picks a free port at boot.
#VIBERR_MCP_PROXY_PORT=0
# Build identity shown on the boot integrity line, /resources/health and the
# controller's instance_health; baked by the image build (Dockerfile ARG, passed
# by compose.yml and filled from git by `npm run deploy`). Leave unset for a
# local build.
#VIBERR_BUILD_VERSION=
#VIBERR_BUILD_SHA=
#VIBERR_BUILD_TIME=

